  "answer_markdown": "For an organization on an **Enterprise plan** that wants new users to join and sign in with corporate credentials only (no separate passwords), the following applies:\n\n1. **Feature to avoid**: **Email domain authentication** should be avoided when using SSO for onboarding. This is because email domain authentication allows users to **create and manage their own credentials** (email and password), which contradicts the goal of using corporate credentials exclusively [1].\n\n2. **Why avoid it**: Email domain authentication is designed for **Team plans and above**, but it does not support SSO-based authentication. Instead, it relies on users managing their own passwords, which defeats the purpose of centralized corporate authentication [1].\n\n3. **What must be verified beforehand for SSO-based onboarding to work**:\n   - The domain must show **Verified** in **Organization Settings** [3].\n   - The **SAML SSO configuration** must be properly set up with a compliant **identity provider (IdP)** like Okta, Azure AD, or Google Workspace [2][4].\n   - **DNS record verification** must be completed to prove domain ownership [2].\n   - **Email-domain authentication** must be enabled for automatic team onboarding (though SSO itself handles authentication, not membership credentials) [2].\n   - The **IdP attribute mapping** must correctly populate user names (first and last) [3].\n\n   To confirm SSO is working, verify:\n   - Team members can sign in via the IdP **without a separate Mistral password** [3].\n   - New users are **automatically added** to the organization upon SSO sign-in [3].",
