{
  "answer_markdown": "Yes, there is a documented maximum number of concurrent token-stream tasks (referred to as **concurrent activity executions**) a single workflow can run, controlled by the parameter `max_concurrent_scheduled_tasks` with a default limit of **100** [1][2][4]. This applies to both the **List Executor** and **Offset Pagination Executor** patterns [1][2][4].\n\nIf this limit is exceeded, the system will **not execute additional tasks beyond the configured limit** until some of the currently running tasks complete [3]. For example, if `max_concurrent_scheduled_tasks=5` and `max_concurrent_executions_per_worker=10`, only **5 groups of 10 items** can be processed concurrently [3].\n\nFor the **Offset Pagination Executor**, there is an additional parameter, `max_concurrent_executions_per_worker`, which controls how many items are processed together in a single activity execution (default: 100) [2][4]. If this limit is exceeded, the entire group of items will be retried together if any item fails [2].",
  "citations": [
    {
      "n": 1,
      "quote": "Maximum number of concurrent activity executions that can be scheduled simultaneously. This limits how many activities are waiting to be executed at once."
